<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "Diet",
      intro: "Elephants are herbivores: they eat only plants. Plants aren't very nutritious, so elephants spend most of their waking hours eating." %>

<% external_link = ->(label, url) { link_to label, url, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "text-link" } %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How much and how often?</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">Food per day</div>
      <div class="stat-value">100–150 kg</div>
      <p>Up to about 300 pounds of plants for an adult.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">Time spent eating</div>
      <div class="stat-value">12–18 hours</div>
      <p>In many small sessions spread across day and night.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">Water per day</div>
      <div class="stat-value">Up to 200 L</div>
      <p>Sometimes 10 litres in a single trunkful.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Why so much?</strong> Elephants digest only about 40% of what they eat. They eat little and often,
      so they're grazing or browsing for most of the day and much of the night.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>What do elephants eat?</h2>
  <p>Follow the links to learn more about each food.</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Food</th>
        <th>Details</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Grasses", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poaceae") %></td>
        <td>The main food in the wet season, when fresh grass is everywhere.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Leaves and twigs", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia") %></td>
        <td>Browsed from trees and shrubs such as acacia, especially in the dry season.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Bark", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bark_(botany)") %></td>
        <td>Stripped from trees with tusks. A good source of fibre and calcium.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Roots", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root") %></td>
        <td>Dug up with feet and tusks when food above ground is scarce.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Fruit", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marula") %></td>
        <td>A favourite treat. Forest elephants eat a lot of fruit, and African elephants love marula.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Bamboo", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo") %></td>
        <td>An important food for Asian elephants in forested areas.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Salt and minerals", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral_lick") %></td>
        <td>Licked or eaten from mineral-rich soil. Some elephants walk into caves to find it.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Diet through the seasons</h2>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      In the rainy season, elephants mostly graze on fresh grass. As the land dries out, grass becomes tough and scarce, so they switch
      to browsing on leaves, branches, bark and roots. This flexibility lets them survive in places with long dry seasons.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Gardeners of the forest</h2>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      Because they digest so little, elephants pass many seeds whole in their dung, sometimes kilometres from the parent plant.
      Some tree species depend almost entirely on elephants to spread their seeds.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
